The plan from here

I would not go straight into "implement SET" next. There are several foundations underneath SET that need to be designed first.

Phase 1 — Connect protocol to the real ESP-NOW transport

Currently we need a clean interface between:

ESP-NOW RX callback
        ↓
receive queue
        ↓
protocol/message task

The master architecture explicitly calls for a lightweight ESP-NOW callback that only pushes frames into a queue, while the Message/Protocol Task performs parsing and request handling.

We will design:

Transport API
peer/MAC table
RX packet queue
TX interface
send-status handling
Node ID ↔ MAC mapping

Crucially, protocol code should not know ESP-NOW details.

Phase 2 — Persistent storage model

Before distributed operations, we need to settle exactly how:

key
value
version
version_origin
tombstone

are represented inside the AT24C256.

The EEPROM is 32 KB and already physically validated, but the project document only specifies the storage boundary, not our final internal record layout.

This is an important design checkpoint.

We'll need to decide something like:

Storage Manager
    ├── put(key, value, version)
    ├── get(key)
    ├── delete/tombstone(key, version)
    └── compare_version(...)

while ensuring:

Only Storage Manager touches I2C.

That boundary is explicitly required by the architecture.

We should design this before distributed replication, because otherwise we'd end up redesigning the EEPROM layout halfway through.

Phase 3 — HLC

Then implement the HLC as its own small module.

We already know the exact semantics; they're frozen:

48-bit physical milliseconds
16-bit logical counter

with the specified local-event and receive-event update rules.

It should expose something conceptually like:

now()
on_local_event()
on_receive(remote_hlc)
compare(...)

and we need the reboot rule tied into persistent storage: after reboot the HLC must start at least as high as the highest persisted mutation version.

This is foundational for every write.

Phase 4 — Request identity + reliability engine

This is the other major foundation.

We'll build:

(origin_node_id, request_id)

as the logical request identity.

Then:

Request Manager
    ├── outgoing requests
    ├── timeout tracking
    ├── retries
    ├── RETRY flag
    ├── response correlation
    └── terminal/late-response handling

and on the receiver:

Dedup Manager
    ├── IN_PROGRESS
    └── COMPLETED + cached result

The key rule is that retries preserve:

origin
request_id
HLC
payload

and only change the retry transmission state/destination as permitted.

We should also keep the timing values configurable. The spec gives 500 ms / 2× backoff / 3 retransmissions / 5 s deadline / 10 s dedup retention as initial values, but explicitly says these are implementation parameters to be measured and retuned.

Phase 5 — Membership / heartbeat

Then:

Heartbeat Task
       ↓
broadcast HEARTBEAT
       ↓
neighbor[node_id] = last_heard_ms

with per-observer liveness, not one global alive[] bitmap.

This gives every node its own current view of the cluster.

We should measure actual radio behavior before permanently choosing heartbeat/failure thresholds; the master document explicitly leaves those values open.

Phase 6 — Consistent-hashing placement

Only once membership exists do we build:

hash(key)
    ↓
ring
    ↓
primary
    ↓
replica

The current architecture is:

4 physical nodes
RF = 2
static ring representation
identical ring computation on every node

and every node independently computes placement.

This module should know about:

Node IDs
membership
ring
key

but not EEPROM or ESP-NOW.

Phase 7 — Actual single-node KV operations

Now we can finally implement the basic semantic operations:

SET
GET
DELETE

without replication initially.

The path should look approximately like:

SET request
   ↓
Protocol decode
   ↓
Request/reliability handling
   ↓
Placement
   ↓
Storage Manager
   ↓
AT24C256
   ↓
versioned result
   ↓
SET_ACK

This is where the status codes become meaningful:

OK
NOT_FOUND
STORAGE_ERROR
NOT_RESPONSIBLE
...

The frozen protocol explicitly distinguishes these application/storage outcomes from transport and parsing failures.

Phase 8 — RF=2 replication

Then add:

Primary
   │
   └── REPL_REQ ──→ Replica
                         │
                         └── REPL_ACK

The important design point is that replication uses the same reliability machinery as normal requests, with the original request identity preserved.

So we shouldn't invent a second retry system specifically for replication.

Phase 9 — Tombstones + version conflict resolution

At this point we make DELETE genuinely distributed:

DELETE
   ↓
tombstone + HLC version
   ↓
replica

and all writes use:

Version = (HLC, version_origin_id)

with lexicographic comparison.

This is what prevents an old replica from resurrecting deleted data.

Phase 10 — Anti-entropy / recovery

Then implement:

VERSION_TABLE
      ↓
compare versions
      ↓
RESYNC_DATA
      ↓
repair stale/missing replica

The frozen design deliberately makes these periodic synchronization messages non-ACKed; dropped sync packets are repaired by future exchanges rather than individual retransmission.

This is also where the under-replicated-write case gets repaired after a failed replica.

Phase 11 — Four-node integration

Only after those pieces work independently:

A ↔ B
A ↔ C
A ↔ D
B ↔ C
B ↔ D
C ↔ D

then:

4 nodes
+
membership
+
ring
+
RF=2
+
failure
+
recovery

We'll deliberately test:

normal write
normal read
delete
retry
duplicate request
node failure
node recovery
stale version
replica failure
anti-entropy repair
power-cycle persistence
Phase 12 — Sensor path

Then connect sensor acquisition:

Sensor Task
    ↓
SET operation
    ↓
same distributed path as everything else

The architecture explicitly wants sensor-originated writes to use the same storage path as network/Pi operations.

Phase 13 — Raspberry Pi bridge

Finally:

Pi
 ↓ UART
Bridge node
 ↓
Message/Protocol Task
 ↓
normal distributed KV path

The Pi does not become a fifth node or coordinator. The bridge is merely a software role on whichever ESP32 is tethered to it.

The frozen protocol has now also sealed the serial wrapper:

AA 55
length
22-byte envelope + payload
CRC-8

so we shouldn't invent another application protocol for the Pi.

The dependency graph

This is the part I would keep in our heads going forward:

                 ┌──────────────┐
                 │ Wire Protocol│ ✅
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
                 ┌──────▼───────┐
                 │ ESP-NOW TX/RX│
                 └──────┬───────┘
                        │
          ┌─────────────┼──────────────┐
          ▼             ▼              ▼
      Storage          HLC       Request/Reliability
          │             │              │
          └─────────────┼──────────────┘
                        ▼
                   Membership
                        │
                        ▼
                 Consistent Hashing
                        │
                        ▼
                  SET/GET/DELETE
                        │
                        ▼
                    RF = 2
                  Replication
                        │
                        ▼
                 Anti-Entropy
                        │
               ┌────────┴────────┐
               ▼                 ▼
            Sensors              Pi

That ordering avoids the worst kind of redesign: building replication before knowing how versions are persisted, or building retries before knowing request identity, or letting the protocol layer become coupled to EEPROM/ESP-NOW.

One thing I would explicitly not build yet

Don't start multi-hop routing, runtime MAC discovery, ESP-NOW v2 fragmentation, or other future-scope features. They're explicitly deferred in v3.

So the next ideation session should be Phase 1: the ESP-NOW transport interface + RX queue + Message/Protocol Task boundary, not SET/GET yet. That gives us the cleanest bridge from the protocol layer we've just finished into the actual firmware architecture.